Thunderbolt 5 + Exo/MLX Distributed LLM Guide
Target setup
```text
Mac Studio A: M3 Ultra, 256 GB unified memory
Mac Studio B: M3 Ultra, 256 GB unified memory
macOS: 26.4
Connection: Thunderbolt 5 direct cable
Goal: distributed language model inference with Apple RDMA over Thunderbolt
Recommended stack: Exo + MLX + JACCL
```
---
0. Important Summary
For your goal:
```text
Mac A runs part of the model
Mac B runs part of the model
256 GB + 256 GB behaves like a larger model pool
```
there are two different paths:
Goal	Best tool
Run GGUF models split across two Macs	`llama.cpp RPC`
Use Apple Thunderbolt RDMA on macOS	`Exo + MLX/JACCL`
GGUF + Apple Thunderbolt RDMA + true model split	Not mature / not cleanly available today
The key distinction:
```text
llama.cpp RPC:
  Good for GGUF memory pooling
  Usually TCP/RPC over Thunderbolt Bridge on macOS
  Not confirmed native Apple RDMA

Exo + MLX/JACCL:
  Best path for Apple RDMA over Thunderbolt
  Uses MLX distributed communication
  Works with MLX/Hugging Face-style models, not GGUF-first
```
---
1. Physical Connection
Use a real Thunderbolt 5 cable, not a USB-C charging cable.
For two Mac Studios:
```text
Mac A <==== Thunderbolt 5 cable ====> Mac B
```
A single direct cable is enough for two nodes.
Mac Studio caveat: avoid the Thunderbolt 5 port next to the Ethernet port if Exo/MLX has issues. Use one of the other Thunderbolt 5 ports.
---
2. Enable Apple RDMA over Thunderbolt
For MLX/JACCL and Exo, RDMA should be enabled from macOS Recovery.
Do this on both Macs.
Shut down the Mac.
Hold the power button until startup options appear.
Choose Options.
Open Utilities → Terminal.
Run:
```bash
rdma_ctl enable
```
Reboot.
After reboot, test:
```bash
ibv_devices
```
Expected style of output:
```text
device      node GUID
------      ----------------
rdma_en2    ...
rdma_en3    ...
rdma_en4    ...
rdma_en5    ...
```
If you see `rdma_en*` devices, Apple RDMA over Thunderbolt is enabled.
---
3. Do Not Treat Thunderbolt Bridge as RDMA
For `llama.cpp RPC`, you often configure:
```text
Thunderbolt Bridge
192.168.50.1
192.168.50.2
```
That is useful for TCP/RPC.
For MLX/JACCL RDMA, TCP/IP is not the actual communication path. MLX/JACCL uses the RDMA interfaces directly, so Thunderbolt Bridge may need to be disabled or isolated depending on the configuration script.
So:
```text
Thunderbolt Bridge fast IP link ≠ proof of RDMA
```
---
4. Easiest Route: Exo macOS App
On both Macs:
```bash
brew install --cask exo
```
Launch Exo on both machines.
Exo may ask for permission to:
```text
modify system network settings
install a network profile
configure RDMA-related interfaces
```
Allow it if you want the automated RDMA setup.
Then open on either Mac:
```text
http://localhost:52415
```
Look for:
```text
2 nodes discovered
both M3 Ultra machines visible
JACCL / RDMA / tensor placement available
```
Good signs:
```text
MlxJaccl
JACCL
RDMA
Tensor
rdma_en*
```
Less ideal signs:
```text
MlxRing
Ring
TCP
only one node
```
`MlxRing` can still work, but it is not the RDMA/JACCL path you want.
---
5. More Controllable Route: Run Exo from Source
Use this route if you want logs and debugging.
5.1 Install dependencies
On both Macs:
```bash
xcode-select --install
brew install uv node git
```
Install Rust nightly:
```bash
curl --proto '=https' --tlsv1.2 -sSf https://sh.rustup.rs | sh
rustup toolchain install nightly
```
Restart your shell, or run:
```bash
source "$HOME/.cargo/env"
```
5.2 Clone Exo
On both Macs:
```bash
git clone https://github.com/exo-explore/exo
cd exo
```
5.3 Build the dashboard
```bash
cd dashboard
npm install
npm run build
cd ..
```
5.4 Configure RDMA network
From the Exo repo:
```bash
sudo tmp/set_rdma_network_config.sh
```
This script is intended to prepare the Thunderbolt/RDMA networking setup used by Exo.
5.5 Run Exo on both Macs
Use the same namespace on both machines:
```bash
EXO_LIBP2P_NAMESPACE=m3u-rdma-cluster \
EXO_TRACING_ENABLED=true \
uv run exo
```
Then open:
```text
http://localhost:52415
```
---
6. Test with a Small Model First
Do not begin with a 200B+ model. First prove that the cluster works.
Example small MLX model:
```text
mlx-community/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct-4bit
```
Preview possible placements:
```bash
curl "http://localhost:52415/instance/previews?model_id=mlx-community/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct-4bit"
```
You want to see a placement involving:
```text
2 nodes
tensor sharding
JACCL / RDMA
```
Then test chat:
```bash
curl -N -X POST http://localhost:52415/v1/chat/completions \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "model": "mlx-community/Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct-4bit",
    "messages": [
      {
        "role": "user",
        "content": "Explain Apple RDMA over Thunderbolt in one paragraph."
      }
    ],
    "stream": true
  }'
```
---
7. Try a Large MLX Model
For your 256 GB + 256 GB setup, a better test is a model that is too large or uncomfortable for one Mac.
Example:
```text
mlx-community/Qwen3-235B-A22B-8bit
```
Add it if needed:
```bash
curl -X POST http://localhost:52415/models/add \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "model_id": "mlx-community/Qwen3-235B-A22B-8bit"
  }'
```
Preview placements:
```bash
curl "http://localhost:52415/instance/previews?model_id=mlx-community/Qwen3-235B-A22B-8bit"
```
Look for a two-node tensor/JACCL placement.
Then request inference through the Exo OpenAI-compatible API:
```bash
curl -N -X POST http://localhost:52415/v1/chat/completions \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{
    "model": "mlx-community/Qwen3-235B-A22B-8bit",
    "messages": [
      {
        "role": "user",
        "content": "Give a concise explanation of mixture-of-experts models."
      }
    ],
    "temperature": 0.2,
    "stream": true
  }'
```
