10. How to Know RDMA Is Really Being Used
Good signs
```bash
ibv_devices
```
shows:
```text
rdma_en2
rdma_en3
rdma_en4
...
```
Exo or MLX logs mention:
```text
JACCL
RDMA
rdma_en*
tensor
MlxJaccl
```
Both Macs show:
```text
high memory use
GPU activity
model shards / placement on both nodes
```
Bad signs
```text
only one Mac is used
placement says Ring/TCP only
no rdma_en* devices
Thunderbolt Bridge IP works but JACCL unavailable
```
Remember:
```text
ping works ≠ RDMA works
iperf3 works ≠ RDMA works
```
`ping` and `iperf3` test IP/TCP networking. JACCL/RDMA is lower-level.
---
11. Where llama.cpp Fits
For GGUF:
```text
llama.cpp RPC is still the best practical distributed option
```
Example:
Mac B
```bash
cd ~/llama.cpp

GGML_RPC_DEBUG=1 ./build/bin/rpc-server \
  -H 192.168.50.2 \
  -p 50052 \
  -c
```
Mac A
```bash
cd ~/llama.cpp

GGML_RPC_DEBUG=1 ./build/bin/llama-server \
  -m /Users/xin/models/large-model.gguf \
  --rpc 192.168.50.2:50052 \
  -ngl all \
  --split-mode layer \
  --tensor-split 1,1 \
  -c 8192 \
  --host 127.0.0.1 \
  --port 8080
```
This can split a GGUF model across both Macs.
But for macOS:
```text
llama.cpp RPC over Thunderbolt: yes
Apple RDMA inside llama.cpp: not confirmed / likely not
```
---
12. Performance Expectations
Best case for Exo/MLX/JACCL
```text
Large MLX model
tensor sharding
two M3 Ultra nodes
Thunderbolt 5 RDMA working
```
Expected benefit:
```text
larger model capacity
better inter-node latency than TCP
better scaling than llama.cpp RPC in many distributed cases
```
Best case for llama.cpp RPC
```text
Large GGUF model
one Mac cannot fit it
two Macs can fit it
```
Expected benefit:
```text
model runs at all
memory pooling works
speed may be lower than single Mac for models that already fit
```
Avoid using two nodes for small models
For models like:
```text
7B
13B
30B
70B quantized
```
one M3 Ultra may be faster because there is no inter-node communication overhead.
Use two nodes mainly for:
```text
100B+
200B+
models that exceed one 256 GB machine
very long context workloads
```
